 ## Background
-The shift from MLP-based NeRFs to grid-based representations was driven by the need for real-time rendering and scalability [G]. SNeRG [S1] precomputed and stored a NeRF model in a sparse 3D voxel grid, using an indirection grid to enhance rendering by skipping empty regions. MERF [S1] presented a significant reduction in memory requirements by leveraging hybrid low-resolution sparse voxels and 2D high-resolution triplanes. KiloNeRF [S2, unofficial] introduced grid distillation, mapping 3D positions to local student networks for fast evaluation. City-on-Web [S1] extended these concepts to large-scale web rendering.
+The shift from implicit MLPs to explicit grids was driven by the need for real-time rendering and scalability. SNeRG and MERF set benchmarks for real-time rendering by using sparse voxel grids and hybrid low-resolution sparse voxels with 2D high-resolution triplanes, respectively [S1]. Hash encoding, popularized by Instant-NGP, introduced multi-resolution hash tables to balance memory and detail [S1]. Large-scale extensions like City-on-Web and Snake-NeRF addressed the memory limits of single-GPU training by partitioning scenes into blocks or tiles [S1].
 
